FYDE: drm/v3d: stop borrowing a foreign drm_file for VC4_CALLOC

import_bo_from_vc4() allocated the CMA buffer through a global
'struct drm_file *vc4_drm_file', which vc5_open() set to whichever
process had most recently opened the vc4 primary node. No reference of
any kind was taken on that file. READ_ONCE() gave the pointer stability
but not lifetime: drm_file_free() runs drm_gem_release(), which
destroys file->object_idr, *before* ->postclose gets to clear the
global. A caller that had already passed the NULL check could therefore
allocate a handle into a torn-down IDR and go on to touch freed slab.
The window is wide -- the function used the file across four separate
operations (create_with_handle, handle_to_fd, its retry, and
handle_delete).

The single global slot was functionally wrong as well. vc5_open()
overwrote it unconditionally on VC4_GEN_6_C, so the last opener won,
and that client's close set it to NULL and made every subsequent
VC4_CALLOC allocation fail with -ENODEV for every other client still
running -- with no fallback in v3d_create_bo_ioctl(), which returns the
error straight to userspace.

None of it was necessary: the buffer only has to be allocated by vc4
because that is where the CMA allocator lives, and that needs the vc4
*device*, not one of its clients. So:

  vc4_dev = vc4_drm_get()        /* new: drm_dev_get() under a mutex */
  drm_dev_enter(vc4_dev, &idx)   /* unbind calls drm_dev_unplug() */
  drm_gem_dma_create(vc4_dev, size)
  drm_gem_prime_export(&dma_obj->base, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC)
  drm_gem_prime_import_dev(dev, dmabuf, dev->dev)
  v3d_obj->dma_buf = dmabuf; get_dma_buf(dmabuf);
  drm_gem_handle_create(file_priv, v3d_obj, handle)

with the resulting ownership chain

  v3d handle -> v3d GEM obj -> import_attach -> dma_buf -> vc4 GEM obj

and the dma_buf additionally holding drm_dev_get() on the vc4 device
(taken by drm_gem_dmabuf_export()), so a buffer stays valid regardless
of what any vc4 client does.

Setting obj->dma_buf by hand is not optional and not cosmetic:
drm_gem_shmem_mmap() passes it straight to dma_buf_mmap() for an
imported BO, so leaving it NULL makes every mmap() of one of these
buffers WARN and return -EINVAL. The stock helper that would set it,
drm_gem_prime_fd_to_handle(), only reaches it by way of a descriptor
installed in the *caller's* fd table; that descriptor stays visible
across several sleeping operations, so a sibling thread could close it
(making our close_fd() shoot down an unrelated descriptor) or dup2()
over it (making the ioctl import a different dma_buf entirely). The
object is not reachable from userspace until the handle exists, so the
assignment here is safe, and drm_gem_handle_create()'s err_unref path
releases the reference for us via
drm_gem_object_exported_dma_buf_free().

The one thing lost relative to drm_gem_prime_fd_to_handle() is the
file_priv->prime cache entry; drm_prime_add_buf_handle() is static to
drm_prime.c and cannot be called from a driver. The effect is limited
to a client that re-imports its own buffer getting a second handle for
it. Re-export still works, because drm_gem_prime_handle_to_fd() takes
the obj->import_attach branch and returns the original dma_buf.

vc4_drm_file, vc5_open() and vc5_close() have no remaining users and
are removed, along with the now-empty .open/.postclose hooks in
vc5_drm_driver. vc4_drm is now published and withdrawn under
vc4_drm_lock and only handed out by vc4_drm_get(), which takes a
reference, closing the window where a caller could be given a pointer
that vc4_drm_unbind() then freed.

While here, return args->offset on this path too. drm_v3d_create_bo
documents it as "Returned offset for the BO in the V3D address space"
that "will always be nonzero, since various HW units treat 0
specially", but the VC4_CALLOC branch returned early without setting
it, and because the ioctl is DRM_IOWR the kernel echoed back whatever
userspace passed in -- zero for the usual zero-initialised struct. The
address was always available; v3d_bo_create_finish() allocates the
drm_mm node during the import. Clients that used
DRM_IOCTL_V3D_GET_BO_OFFSET instead are unaffected.

Also drop the de-static/EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL of
drm_gem_dma_create_with_handle() and its declaration in
include/drm/drm_gem_dma_helper.h: it was widened for the old
implementation and now has no users outside its own file. It is
precisely the footgun this fix removes -- it installs a GEM handle for
a vc4 object into whatever drm_file it is passed.

